🚨 One suggestion

I want to make one change compared to Week 1.

Last time, we built the project from scratch.

This time, let's behave like you're maintaining an existing application.

That means we'll first build a working Student Grade Management System, then intentionally create a separate buggy copy by introducing realistic defects. This makes the debugging process authentic—you can clearly show the original bug, reproduce it, fix it, and compare the before/after behavior.

It's a much stronger story for your report and aligns better with the task's emphasis on debugging rather than initial development.

So we'll have three versions conceptually:

Original working version (used only during development)
Buggy version (the one we'll analyze and debug)
Fixed version (the final corrected deliverable)

The report will compare the buggy and fixed versions, making your debugging process much more convincing. I think this approach will score better than simply writing buggy code from the beginning.